research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10954-          ]
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10955-        },
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10956-        {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10957-          "fact": "L5",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10958-          "source": "fs-limit-equals-value",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10959-          "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json:10960:          "quote": "**False claim:** if $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, if $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$, if\n$c \\in A$ is a limit point of $A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) and if the limit of $f$\nat $c$ exists ([[def-function-limit]]), then\n\n$$\\lim_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c) .$$\n\nBoth sides of the asserted equation are defined under the stated hypotheses: the\nleft because the limit is assumed to exist and is single valued\n([[lem-function-limit-unique]]), the right because $c \\in A$. The claim is that\nthey always agree, and that is false.\n\n**Why it is tempting.** The condition $|f(x) - L| < \\varepsilon$ is imposed on\npoints $x$ arbitrarily close to $c$, and it feels as though $x = c$ were the\nlimiting case of that. It is not: [[def-function-limit]] quantifies over\n$0 < |x - c| < \\delta$, and the strict inequality on the left removes $x = c$\nfrom the quantifier entirely. Changing the value of $f$ at the single point $c$\ntherefore changes nothing on the left-hand side and everything on the right.\n\n**What is true.** The equation above is not a theorem but a *condition*, and it\nis the condition the next page of this track takes as the definition of\ncontinuity at $c$. This library states it as a hypothesis and never as a\nconsequence; hypothesis (i) of [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]] is exactly\nthis condition for the outer function.",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10961-          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10962-            "1.2",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10963-            "3.1",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10964-            "3.2"
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10965-          ]
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10966-        },
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10967-        {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10968-          "fact": "L6",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json:10969:          "source": "thm-composition-of-function-limits",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10970-          "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10971-          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g(A) \\subseteq B$, and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c$ be a limit point of $A$ and\n$L$ a limit point of $B$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]), and suppose the limits\n\n$$\\lim_{x \\to c} g(x) = L \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{y \\to L} f(y) = M$$\n\nboth exist, with the stated values ([[def-function-limit]]). Suppose in addition\nthat **at least one** of the following holds:\n\n- **(i)** $L \\in B$ and $f(L) = M$;\n- **(ii)** there is a real $\\eta > 0$ with $g(x) \\ne L$ for every $x \\in A$\n  satisfying $0 < |x - c| < \\eta$.\n\nThen the limit of $f \\circ g$ at $c$ **exists**, and\n\n$$\\lim_{x \\to c} f\\bigl(g(x)\\bigr) \\;=\\; \\lim_{y \\to L} f(y) \\;=\\; M .$$\n\n**At least one extra hypothesis is necessary.** With both omitted the statement is\nfalse, and [[fs-naive-composition-of-limits]] refutes it with a two-line witness\nin which (i) fails because $f(L) \\ne M$ and (ii) fails because $g$ is constantly\nequal to $L$.\n\n**Why an extra hypothesis is needed at all.** The inner limit controls $g(x)$\nonly up to $|g(x) - L| < \\rho$; it does not prevent $g(x)$ from *equalling* $L$.\nBut [[def-function-limit]] says nothing about $f$ at the point $L$, so the outer\nestimate is unavailable exactly at the values $g(x) = L$. Hypothesis (i) supplies\nthe missing value directly; hypothesis (ii) excludes those values.",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10972-          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10973-            "3.2"
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10974-          ]
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10975-        }
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-completeness-limits.proof-contracts.json-10976-      ],
