research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-151-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-152-**Given:** The sets $A := \mathbb{R}$ and $B := \mathbb{R}$; the point $c := 0$; the function $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ of [[fs-limit-equals-value]], namely $f(y) := 0$ for $y \ne 0$ and $f(0) := 1$; and the constant function $g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$, $g(x) := 0$ for every $x$.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-153-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-154-[L1] The limit condition ([[def-function-limit]]): $\lim_{x \to c} h(x) = P$ means that for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a real $\delta > 0$ such that every $x$ in the domain of $h$ with $0 < |x - c| < \delta$ satisfies $|h(x) - P| < \varepsilon$.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-155-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-156-[L2] Every real is a limit point of $\mathbb{R}$, punctured neighbourhoods being never empty ([[def-limit-point-r]], [[def-neighbourhood-r]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-157-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-158-[L3] Absolute value: $|0| = 0$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-159-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-160-[L4] Order in $\mathbb{R}$: trichotomy, and $0 < 1$, so $1 \ne 0$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-161-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-naive-composition-of-limits.prompt.md-162-[L5] The function $f$ above satisfies $f(0) = 1$ and has limit $0$ at $0$: for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ the radius $\delta = 1$ works, since $0 < |y - 0| < 1$ forces $y \ne 0$ and then $|f(y) - 0| = 0 < \varepsilon$; this is the computation carried out in [[fs-limit-equals-value]].
