research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-252-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-253-[L5] Order in $\mathbb{R}$: trichotomy, and $0 < 1$, so $1 \ne 0$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-254-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md:255:[L6] Composition of limits, and its two extra hypotheses: (i) $L \in B$ and $f(L) = M$; (ii) some real $\eta > 0$ has $g(x) \ne L$ for every $x \in A$ with $0 < |x - c| < \eta$ ([[thm-composition-of-function-limits]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-256-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-257-## Counterexample
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-258-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-259-**Proof technique:** direct.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-260-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-261-1.1 By [L3] the limit of $f$ at $0$ exists and equals $0$, and $f(0) = 1$; so the outer hypothesis of the refuted claim holds with $L = 0$ and $M = 0$. [L3]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-262-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-263-1.2 $0$ is a limit point of $\mathbb{R}$, and $g(\mathbb{R}) = \{0\} \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ and $\iota(\mathbb{R}) = \mathbb{R}$, so both $f \circ g$ and $f \circ \iota$ are functions on $\mathbb{R}$. [L2]
--
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-269-2.2 It holds for $\iota$ as well: given a real $\varepsilon > 0$ take $\delta := \varepsilon$; then $0 < |x - 0| < \delta$ gives $|\iota(x) - 0| = |x| < \varepsilon$. So the limit of $\iota$ at $0$ exists and equals $0$. [step 1.2, L1]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-270-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-271-3.1 $f \circ g$ is the constant function $1$: for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$, $g(x) = 0$ and hence $f(g(x)) = f(0) = 1$. By the computation of step 2.1, applied to the constant $1$ in place of the constant $0$, the limit of $f \circ g$ at $0$ exists and equals $1$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L1, L4]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-272-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-273-3.2 $f \circ \iota = f$, since $f(\iota(x)) = f(x)$ for every $x$; so by [L3] the limit of $f \circ \iota$ at $0$ exists and equals $0 = M$. [step 1.1, step 2.2, L3]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-274-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-275-4.1 Hence $\lim_{x \to 0} g(x) = 0 = L$ and $\lim_{y \to 0} f(y) = 0 = M$, while $\lim_{x \to 0} f(g(x)) = 1 \ne 0 = M$: the refuted claim is false. [step 1.3, step 3.1, L5]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-276-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md:277:4.2 Both extra hypotheses of [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]] fail for the pair $(f, g)$: hypothesis (i) fails because $L = 0$ lies in $B = \mathbb{R}$ while $f(L) = f(0) = 1 \ne 0 = M$, and hypothesis (ii) fails because $g(x) = 0 = L$ for every $x$, so no punctured neighbourhood of $0$ avoids the value $L$. For the pair $(f, \iota)$, hypothesis (ii) does hold with $\eta := 1$, since $\iota(x) = x \ne 0$ whenever $0 < |x - 0| < 1$; and step 3.2 confirms the conclusion of the theorem there. [step 1.1, step 3.1, step 3.2, L6]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-278-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-279-5.1 So the two safeguards in the true theorem cannot both be omitted, and the obstruction is located exactly at the values of the inner function that equal $L$. [step 4.1, step 4.2] ∎
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-280-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-281-## Remarks
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-282-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-283-- **The same outer function serves both roles.** With $g$ the composition fails, with $\iota$ it succeeds, and $f$ is unchanged. So the failure cannot be attributed to any pathology of $f$ beyond the one recorded in [[cex-limit-differs-from-the-value]]: that its value at $0$ differs from its limit at $0$.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-284-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-285-- **Constancy of $g$ is not the issue either.** What matters is that $g$ takes the value $L$ on every punctured neighbourhood of $c$. Any inner function doing that, constant or not, produces the same failure by the same argument, since the outer estimate is unavailable at those arguments.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-286-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md:287:- **The practical rule.** When substituting $y = g(x)$ inside a limit, check one of the two hypotheses of [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]]: either the outer function is defined at $L$ with the right value there, or the inner function avoids $L$ near $c$. Substitutions such as $y = 1/x$ satisfy the second for structural reasons; substitutions into a function known only through its limit satisfy neither in general.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-288-````
