research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-664-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-665-- **A removable defect, not a jump.** By step 2.2 the two one-sided limits exist and agree with each other and with the two-sided limit; the only disagreement is with the value. Compare the sign function ([[ex-one-sided-limits-of-the-sign-function]]), where the two one-sided limits exist and *disagree*, and no redefinition of the value can repair anything.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-666-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md:667:- **Why the witness is used again for composition.** Because $f(0) \ne \lim_{y \to 0} f(y)$, hypothesis (i) of [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]] fails for $f$ at $L = 0$; feeding it an inner function that takes the value $0$ then breaks the composition, which is [[cex-composition-of-limits-fails]].
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-668-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-669-- **Nothing here depends on the particular values $0$ and $1$**, only on their being distinct. Any function constant off $c$ with a different value at $c$ refutes the claim in the same three lines.
