research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1205-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1206-**Given:** The sets $A := \mathbb{R}$ and $B := \mathbb{R}$; the point $c := 0$; the function $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ of [[fs-limit-equals-value]], namely $f(y) := 0$ for $y \ne 0$ and $f(0) := 1$; and the constant function $g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$, $g(x) := 0$ for every $x$.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1207-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1208-[L1] The limit condition ([[def-function-limit]]): $\lim_{x \to c} h(x) = P$ means that for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a real $\delta > 0$ such that every $x$ in the domain of $h$ with $0 < |x - c| < \delta$ satisfies $|h(x) - P| < \varepsilon$.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1209-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1210-[L2] Every real is a limit point of $\mathbb{R}$, punctured neighbourhoods being never empty ([[def-limit-point-r]], [[def-neighbourhood-r]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1211-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1212-[L3] Absolute value: $|0| = 0$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1213-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1214-[L4] Order in $\mathbb{R}$: trichotomy, and $0 < 1$, so $1 \ne 0$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1215-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1216-[L5] The function $f$ above satisfies $f(0) = 1$ and has limit $0$ at $0$: for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ the radius $\delta = 1$ works, since $0 < |y - 0| < 1$ forces $y \ne 0$ and then $|f(y) - 0| = 0 < \varepsilon$; this is the computation carried out in [[fs-limit-equals-value]].
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1217-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md:1218:[L6] Composition of limits and its two extra hypotheses (i) and (ii) ([[thm-composition-of-function-limits]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1219-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1220-## Refutation
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1221-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1222-**Proof technique:** direct.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1223-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1224-1.1 The point $0$ is a limit point of $\mathbb{R}$, and $g(\mathbb{R}) = \{0\} \subseteq \mathbb{R} = B$, so $f \circ g$ is a function on $\mathbb{R}$. [L2]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1225-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1226-1.2 By [L5], $\lim_{y \to 0} f(y) = 0$; so the outer hypothesis holds with $L = 0$ and $M = 0$. [L5]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1227-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1228-1.3 The reals $0$ and $1$ are distinct. [L4]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1229-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1230-2.1 The inner hypothesis holds with $L = 0$: for the constant function $g$ and any real $\varepsilon > 0$, every $\delta > 0$ works, since $|g(x) - 0| = |0| = 0 < \varepsilon$ for every $x$. So $\lim_{x \to 0} g(x) = 0$. [step 1.1, L1, L3]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1231-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1232-3.1 But $f \circ g$ is the constant function $1$: for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$, $g(x) = 0$ and hence $f(g(x)) = f(0) = 1$. Therefore, by the same computation as in step 2.1, the limit of $f \circ g$ at $0$ exists and equals $1$. [step 2.1, L1, L3, L5]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1233-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md:1234:3.2 Both extra hypotheses of [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]] fail for this pair: hypothesis (i) fails because $L = 0$ lies in $B = \mathbb{R}$ while $f(L) = f(0) = 1 \ne 0 = M$; and hypothesis (ii) fails because $g(x) = 0 = L$ for every $x$, so no punctured neighbourhood of $0$ avoids the value $L$. [step 2.1, L5, L6]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1235-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1236-4.1 So $\lim_{x \to 0} g(x) = 0 = L$ and $\lim_{y \to 0} f(y) = 0 = M$, while $\lim_{x \to 0} f(g(x)) = 1 \ne 0 = M$: the claim is false, and step 3.2 identifies exactly which hypotheses of the true theorem are missing. [step 1.2, step 1.3, step 3.1, step 3.2] ∎
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1237-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1238-## Remarks
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1239-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1240-- **The failure is not an artefact of the constant inner function.** What matters is that $g$ takes the value $L$ on every punctured neighbourhood of $c$; a non-constant $g$ that hits $L$ along a sequence tending to $c$ would fail in the same way. Conversely, replacing $g$ by the identity — which avoids the value $0$ off the point $0$ itself — restores the conclusion, and the companion page carries out that comparison.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1241-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md:1242:- **Textbook statements almost always assume continuity of the outer function**, which is hypothesis (i) of [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]] written out. The version with hypothesis (ii) is the one that licenses substitutions such as $y = 1/x$, where the inner function omits the critical value for a structural reason.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1243-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-composition-of-limits-fails.prompt.md-1244-- **The same witness refutes nothing else on this page.** In particular it does not bear on [[thm-algebra-of-function-limits]]: sums, products and quotients are formed pointwise from the values of $f$ and $g$ at the *same* argument, and no composition is involved.
