research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-limit-equals-value.prompt.md:1026:3.2 Both extra hypotheses of [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]] fail for this pair: hypothesis (i) fails because $L = 0$ lies in $B = \mathbb{R}$ while $f(L) = f(0) = 1 \ne 0 = M$; and hypothesis (ii) fails because $g(x) = 0 = L$ for every $x$, so no punctured neighbourhood of $0$ avoids the value $L$. [step 2.1, L5, L6]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-limit-equals-value.prompt.md-1027-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-limit-equals-value.prompt.md-1028-4.1 So $\lim_{x \to 0} g(x) = 0 = L$ and $\lim_{y \to 0} f(y) = 0 = M$, while $\lim_{x \to 0} f(g(x)) = 1 \ne 0 = M$: the claim is false, and step 3.2 identifies exactly which hypotheses of the true theorem are missing. [step 1.2, step 1.3, step 3.1, step 3.2] ∎
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-limit-equals-value.prompt.md-1029-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-limit-equals-value.prompt.md-1030-## Remarks
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-limit-equals-value.prompt.md-1031-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-limit-equals-value.prompt.md-1032-- **The failure is not an artefact of the constant inner function.** What matters is that $g$ takes the value $L$ on every punctured neighbourhood of $c$; a non-constant $g$ that hits $L$ along a sequence tending to $c$ would fail in the same way. Conversely, replacing $g$ by the identity — which avoids the value $0$ off the point $0$ itself — restores the conclusion, and the companion page carries out that comparison.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-limit-equals-value.prompt.md-1033-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-limit-equals-value.prompt.md:1034:- **Textbook statements almost always assume continuity of the outer function**, which is hypothesis (i) of [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]] written out. The version with hypothesis (ii) is the one that licenses substitutions such as $y = 1/x$, where the inner function omits the critical value for a structural reason.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-limit-equals-value.prompt.md-1035-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-fs-limit-equals-value.prompt.md-1036-- **The same witness refutes nothing else on this page.** In particular it does not bear on [[thm-algebra-of-function-limits]]: sums, products and quotients are formed pointwise from the values of $f$ and $g$ at the *same* argument, and no composition is involved.
