research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-652-2.2 It holds for $\iota$ as well: given a real $\varepsilon > 0$ take $\delta := \varepsilon$; then $0 < |x - 0| < \delta$ gives $|\iota(x) - 0| = |x| < \varepsilon$. So the limit of $\iota$ at $0$ exists and equals $0$. [step 1.2, L1]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-653-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-654-3.1 $f \circ g$ is the constant function $1$: for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$, $g(x) = 0$ and hence $f(g(x)) = f(0) = 1$. By the computation of step 2.1, applied to the constant $1$ in place of the constant $0$, the limit of $f \circ g$ at $0$ exists and equals $1$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L1, L4]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-655-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-656-3.2 $f \circ \iota = f$, since $f(\iota(x)) = f(x)$ for every $x$; so by [L3] the limit of $f \circ \iota$ at $0$ exists and equals $0 = M$. [step 1.1, step 2.2, L3]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-657-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-658-4.1 Hence $\lim_{x \to 0} g(x) = 0 = L$ and $\lim_{y \to 0} f(y) = 0 = M$, while $\lim_{x \to 0} f(g(x)) = 1 \ne 0 = M$: the refuted claim is false. [step 1.3, step 3.1, L5]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-659-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md:660:4.2 Both extra hypotheses of [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]] fail for the pair $(f, g)$: hypothesis (i) fails because $L = 0$ lies in $B = \mathbb{R}$ while $f(L) = f(0) = 1 \ne 0 = M$, and hypothesis (ii) fails because $g(x) = 0 = L$ for every $x$, so no punctured neighbourhood of $0$ avoids the value $L$. For the pair $(f, \iota)$, hypothesis (ii) does hold with $\eta := 1$, since $\iota(x) = x \ne 0$ whenever $0 < |x - 0| < 1$; and step 3.2 confirms the conclusion of the theorem there. [step 1.1, step 3.1, step 3.2, L6]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-661-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-662-5.1 So the two safeguards in the true theorem cannot both be omitted, and the obstruction is located exactly at the values of the inner function that equal $L$. [step 4.1, step 4.2] ∎
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-663-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-664-## Remarks
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-665-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-666-- **The same outer function serves both roles.** With $g$ the composition fails, with $\iota$ it succeeds, and $f$ is unchanged. So the failure cannot be attributed to any pathology of $f$ beyond the one recorded in [[cex-limit-differs-from-the-value]]: that its value at $0$ differs from its limit at $0$.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-667-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-668-- **Constancy of $g$ is not the issue either.** What matters is that $g$ takes the value $L$ on every punctured neighbourhood of $c$. Any inner function doing that, constant or not, produces the same failure by the same argument, since the outer estimate is unavailable at those arguments.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md-669-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-limit-differs-from-the-value.prompt.md:670:- **The practical rule.** When substituting $y = g(x)$ inside a limit, check one of the two hypotheses of [[thm-composition-of-function-limits]]: either the outer function is defined at $L$ with the right value there, or the inner function avoids $L$ near $c$. Substitutions such as $y = 1/x$ satisfy the second for structural reasons; substitutions into a function known only through its limit satisfy neither in general.
